    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Is this a good offer?\n\n**Yes, as an opening offer, it is solid.**\n\n* **Market positioning:** At Google, the median Total Compensation (TC) for an L6 Product Manager in the Bay Area typically hovers between **$470,000 and $530,000**. An average annualized TC of ~$552,000 puts this offer in the top quartile for the level.\n* **The front-loaded vesting reality:** Google\u2019s standard new-hire equity vesting schedule is front-loaded at **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%**:\n  * **Year 1:** $235,000 base + $47,000 bonus + $356,400 equity (33%) = **~$638,400**\n  * **Year 2:** $235,000 base + $47,000 bonus + $356,400 equity (33%) = **~$638,400** *(+ year 1 refresher vest)*\n  * **Year 3:** $235,000 base + $47,000 bonus + $237,600 equity (22%) = **~$519,600** *(+ stacked refreshers)*\n  * **Year 4:** $235,000 base + $47,000 bonus + $129,600 equity (12%) = **~$411,600** *(+ stacked refreshers)*\n\nCompared to your current ~$526,000/yr at Meta (and specifically the ~$476,000 you would earn over the next 12 months with $200k in RSUs vesting), Google\u2019s Year 1 and Year 2 compensation represents a clear increase.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Should you accept, negotiate, or walk away?\n\n**You should definitely negotiate.** \n\n* **Do not accept as-is:** Google almost always expects a counter-offer, especially at L6.\n* **Do not walk away:** You have near-perfect leverage:\n  1. You were **passively sourced** and are happy in your current role.\n  2. You hold a direct equivalent role (**Meta IC6**) at Google\u2019s top talent competitor.\n  3. They have offered **$0 in sign-on bonus**, which is the easiest lever for a Google compensation committee to approve.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to negotiate for and how much\n\nWhen countering, aim for a balanced package that pushes your 4-year grant and adds an upfront cash bridge:\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Target Counter | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$245,000 \u2013 $250,000** | Protects your current $240k base at Meta; well within Google\u2019s posted band ($183k\u2013$271k). |\n| **New-Hire RSUs** | $1,080,000 | **$1,200,000 \u2013 $1,250,000** | Pushes your average annual equity to ~$300k/yr and cushions the Year 3/4 drop-off. |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$60,000 \u2013 $80,000** | Offsets leaving Meta mid-cycle / unvested equity cadence and bridges any timing risk. |\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to frame the negotiation\n\nKeep it collaborative, positive, and anchored on your leverage as a happy, well-compensated Meta IC6:\n\n> *\"Thank you for the offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m excited about the scope of this L6 role.*\n> \n> *Because I wasn't looking to move and am in a great spot at Meta, compensation parity and upside are critical for me to make the switch. In particular, the offer slightly dips below my current base salary ($240k), and without a sign-on bonus, leaving my current vesting schedule creates some friction.*\n> \n> *If we can get the base to **$245,000**, increase the initial equity grant to **$1.2M**, and include a **$75,000 sign-on bonus**, I would be thrilled to sign immediately.\"*\n\n### Key Caveat to Keep in Mind\nGoogle\u2019s recruiter was correct that **refreshers are not guaranteed** and are tied to performance ratings (which have tightened across Big Tech). This is another reason to maximize the **initial 4-year grant** and **upfront sign-on cash**, as those are guaranteed contractually regardless of future performance calibration cycles.",
